{# Site navigation.

   The mobile drawer is a checkbox + label pair styled by sibling selectors,
   so it opens and closes with scripting disabled. The search field sits
   between the wordmark and the links, and is hidden by CSS until
   search_bar.js marks <html> — see section I of input.css.

   Two things here now want scripting, and neither is the navigation itself.
   The search field is one; the other is nav_reveal.js, which holds this bar
   back over a page that opens with a hero until the reader is about half a
   viewport into it. Both fail the same way: with scripting off the field is
   never shown and the bar is never held back, so what is left is a complete
   nav bar with links that work. Nothing in this file is conditional on it —
   the markup is identical on every page, and the reveal is entirely a matter
   of two classes on <html> and the rules in section F that read them.

   That reveal runs at every width, phones included, and the menu button below
   goes with the rest of the row when it does. So anything put inside
   .nav-inner is off screen while a leading hero owns the page: a control a
   reader might need before they have scrolled does not belong in here. #}
{% import "search.njk" as search %}
<header class="site-nav">
  <div class="shell">
    <input class="nav-toggle" type="checkbox" id="nav-toggle" aria-label="Open navigation">

    <div class="nav-inner">
      {# The wordmark is a nav link: .nav-link gives it the same font, size,
         tracking and optical baseline nudge as the links opposite, so the bar
         reads as one typographic row.

         Monochrome, unlike the footer wordmark, which keeps .text-flow. The
         mark is a mask painted with currentColor (see .site-logo in input.css),
         so it can only ever be one colour — and a gradient name beside a flat
         mark reads as two objects that happen to be adjacent. Dropping the
         gradient here makes the pair one lockup. The name is still held a step
         above the other links, at --fg against their --fg-muted; that is
         .nav-wordmark's job now rather than the gradient's.

         The mark leads and the name follows, both inside the one anchor, so
         either half is the same home link with the same hover. #}
      <a class="nav-link nav-wordmark" href="/" aria-label="{{ settings.name }} — home">
        {# Hidden from assistive tech: the anchor already carries an aria-label
           and prints the site name in text beside this, so an announced mark
           would name the same link twice. It is decoration on a labelled
           control — and, being a mask, it has no content to announce anyway. #}
        <span class="site-logo" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {{ settings.short_name }}
      </a>

      {# The wrapper absorbs the free space and pushes the field to its right
         edge, so the search sits immediately left of the links rather than
         floating in the middle of the bar. #}
      <div class="nav-desktop-only" style="flex:1 1 auto; justify-content:flex-end;">
        {{ search.field("desktop", settings) }}
      </div>

      <nav class="nav-desktop-only" aria-label="Primary" style="gap:1.75rem; align-items:center;">
        {% for item in settings.nav %}
        <a class="nav-link" href="{{ item.link }}"{% if page.url == item.link %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a>
        {% endfor %}
      </nav>

      {# Both states ship in the markup and CSS swaps which one is displayed,
         so the button changes with the checkbox alone and still needs no
         JavaScript. The glyphs are masked files from svg/ — see .icon in
         input.css — not inline paths, so the bar and the rest of the site draw
         their icons from the same source. #}
      <label class="nav-toggle-label" for="nav-toggle">
        <span class="icon icon-menu nav-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="icon icon-cross nav-toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="sr-only">Menu</span>
      </label>
    </div>

    {# Mobile drawer. Duplicated links rather than a moved node, because CSS
       cannot relocate an element and this must work without JavaScript.

       The drawer floats over the page rather than pushing it down, so it needs
       the scrim below it: without one, everything the open menu does not
       happen to cover stays live, and a tap meant to dismiss the menu lands on
       a link instead. #}
    <nav class="nav-drawer" aria-label="Primary, mobile">
      {{ search.field("drawer", settings) }}
      {% for item in settings.nav %}
      <a class="nav-link" href="{{ item.link }}"{% if page.url == item.link %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a>
      {% endfor %}
    </nav>

    {# Tap-outside-to-close, and the shield that makes the overlay safe. A
       second <label> for the same checkbox, so dismissing the menu needs no
       more scripting than opening it did.

       Hidden from assistive tech: it is a pointer affordance for a control
       that is already in the page and already labelled. A screen reader user
       leaves the menu by returning to the toggle, and announcing a nameless
       second control for the same checkbox would only be noise. Safe to hide,
       because a <label> is not focusable — nothing is being removed from the
       keyboard path. #}
    <label class="nav-scrim" for="nav-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></label>
  </div>
</header>
